---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="How Top Picks ranks web hosting"
  description="The 2026 ranking method: public pricing, time-to-first-site, renewal honesty, and a path to grow. Not a performance lab."
  path="/methodology"
>
  <main class="wrap prose">
    <div class="kicker">Methodology</div>
    <h1>How the ranking is built</h1>
    <p>
      Top Picks is a comparison site, not a synthetic-benchmark lab. We do not claim
      to have measured every host’s TTFB from five continents, and we did not receive
      review units from vendors. The score is an editorial judgment from information
      anyone can check: advertised price, known renewal behavior, who the product is
      built for, and how long it takes a non-specialist to get a page on the internet.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a limitation. It is also more honest than a “9.8” produced by a spreadsheet
      nobody published. If you need packet-level performance data, look at independent
      uptime trackers and run your own stack. This ranking is for purchase decisions.
    </p>
    <h2>What weighs more</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Time to a live site.</strong> Can someone with no sysadmin background
        buy a plan, attach a domain, turn on SSL, and publish this week.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Price honesty.</strong> Is the advertised number close to what they
        will pay in month 13, or is the homepage a 48-month teaser.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A path to grow.</strong> When the brochure site becomes a shop, or the
        shop becomes an app, can they scale without a panicked weekend migration.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Those three filters are why Hostinger is first for most people and Hetzner is
      first for anyone who already knows SSH. One list cannot maximize both audiences.
      That is why every row has a badge: the score is relative to that job, not a
      universal quality medal.
    </p>
    <h2>What we do not score (yet)</h2>
    <p>
      Support wait times, exact uptime, and raw CPU benchmarks are not in the number.
      Those change by plan, region, and week. Where a limitation is well known — renewal
      jumps, missing one-click WordPress, expensive bandwidth — it shows up in the
      review as a con, not as a fake precision score.
    </p>
    <h2>Money</h2>
    <p>
      Some links are affiliate links. Order is not set by commission alone, but we do
      have an incentive for clicks. If that conflict matters to you, read the
      <a href="/disclosure">disclosure</a> before you use the buttons.
    </p>
    <p>
      Prices on this site were checked against public marketing pages in October 2026.
      Checkout can disagree. Treat our numbers as a starting point, not an invoice.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
